        "Nicholas B. Lydon",
        "Charles L. Sawyers"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "BCR-ABL inhibition provided a defining demonstration that a genetically specified cancer driver could be targeted to yield dramatic clinical benefit.",
        "attribution": "Lydon, Druker, and Sawyers represent drug discovery, clinical translation, and the biological understanding of response and resistance.",
        "maturity": "The therapeutic principle has been repeatedly validated over decades and has influenced targeted oncology broadly.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery rests on prior identification of BCR-ABL and Philadelphia-chromosome biology, creating an unavoidable attribution boundary."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Jacques F. A. P. Miller",
        "Max D. Cooper"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The recognition of distinct T- and B-lymphocyte lineages established a central organizing principle of adaptive immunity.",
        "attribution": "Miller and Cooper make a compact and scientifically coherent attribution for the respective thymus-dependent and bursa-derived lymphocyte lineages.",
        "maturity": "The findings have been foundational to immunology for many decades and have been reinforced by subsequent cellular and molecular work.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The historical experimental literature involves several related contributors and model-system-specific interpretations."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
